==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|æquivocate}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|aequivocatus|aequivocātus|lang=la}}, perfect passive participle of {{term|aequivoco|aequivocō|I am called by the same name|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|aequivocus||ambiguous, equivocal|lang=la}}: confer {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|équivoquer|lang=fr}}. See [[equivocal]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ɪˈkwɪvəˌkeɪt/}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|equivocat|ing}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To use words of equivocal or doubtful signification; to express one's opinions in terms which admit of different senses, with intent to deceive; to use ambiguous expressions with a view to mislead; as, to equivocate is the work of duplicity.
#: ''All that Garnet had to say for him was that he supposed he meant to '''equivocate'''.'' -[[w:Edward Stillingfleet|Edward Stillingfleet]].
# To render equivocal or ambiguous.
#: ''He '''equivocated''' his vow by a mental reservation.'' -[[w:George Buck|George Buck]].

